/id #edb/ref 17592186305255} {:answer/id #uuid "316d241a-8a8a-5270-84e3-f0fc3ef40261" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "adding the vectors ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305617} {:answer/id #uuid "39f356a8-8fda-5c4b-9454-f7cb76d1ea0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "integrating the given system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305702} {:answer/id #uuid "600321e5-9661-57d4-a3e1-64da86996880" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "multiplying $Φ(t)$ with it's inverse" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306076} {:answer/id #uuid "e313c524-01fc-5609-ae4c-14eafceafe00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the definition of a basis" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307255}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c29a02a-5ddf-5ffe-8562-988f1e1a9aff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "the definition of a basis" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305255} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bd31540-fe17-5fac-bdc9-cc5602d470d7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308012} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9df8332a-0028-5851-a541-699e5fb5b445" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "definition of matrix multiplication" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306628} {:answer/id #uuid "a35b581b-96f5-5832-8aa2-3b756a1be5cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "definition of a fundamental matrix" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306689} {:answer/id #uuid "b096b37f-397b-59cd-b45a-7a82f14b6d6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "fact that $Φ$ is nonsingular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306813} {:answer/id #uuid "edcd7607-2741-5c34-acfc-0d847398cbb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "properties of invertible matrices" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307336} {:answer/id #uuid "edd1095a-2f45-5472-aa18-be05cd67e567" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "definition of matrix multiplication" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307337}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "edd1095a-2f45-5472-aa18-be05cd67e567" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "definition of matrix multiplication" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307337} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5be50c8-6e0b-5b31-b6e5-a6b8db5b8b63" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308245}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7179862e-a345-5508-93d4-73732015d190" :question/math-academy-id "q-333723" :question/problem "Consider the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant $2\\times 2$ matrix. Let $Φ(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for this system. A derivation showing that every solution $x(t)$ can be written in the form $x(t)=Φ(t)\\,c$ for some constant vector $c=[{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}]^{T}$ is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ be the columns of $Φ(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ form a basis of solutions\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $x(t)$ can be written as $x(t)={c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $Φ(t)\\,c={c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $x(t)=Φ(t)\\,c$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from line $\\text{L2}$ by {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ by the {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}.$ Since $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ form a basis of the solution space for our system. Thus, $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the $\\text{definition of a fundamental matrix}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}.$ Once ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ form a basis, every solution $x(t)$ of the system can be expressed uniquely as a linear combination\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ by $\\text{the definition of a basis}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L4}.$ Since ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are the columns of $Φ(t),$ we can write\n\n$$\nΦ(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\,| & | \\\\ {x}_{1}(t) & {x}_{2}(t) \\\\ \\,| & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThen by the definition of matrix multiplication,\n\n$$\nΦ(t)\\,c=\\begin{bmatrix}\\,| & | \\\\ {x}_{1}(t) & {x}_{2}(t) \\\\ \\,| & |\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L4}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the $\\text{definition of matrix multiplication}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398781 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221420 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "af871f3d-da9e-5fd3-a61f-94e155ccf2b3" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Fundamental Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c007ca0-acef-5af6-b3fe-264373817071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{-1} & 2{e}^{-1} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-1}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398783} {:answer/id #uuid "59ce7ec4-615c-508f-9c8e-4b5a5d18d797" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} & 0 \\\\ 2t{e}^{-t} & 2{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398784} {:answer/id #uuid "b333daa7-d3c9-5885-83dd-c52477a27945" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2t \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398785} {:answer/id #uuid "1281b7c7-c44e-5d28-ae85-689e0d3ae235" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{-t} & 2t{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398786} {:answer/id #uuid "de8dcb0a-6338-525e-8932-5efde71571f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398787}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1281b7c7-c44e-5d28-ae85-689e0d3ae235" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{-t} & 2t{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398786} :answer-field/id #uuid "f91e0298-1d33-5125-a1ef-22a1c3b27757" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398782}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6ce970ec-3f9d-57d6-9307-1253ccd1b689" :question/math-academy-id "q-334326" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}x,\\,{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs and two of its linearly independent solutions ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ shown above. Which of the following is a fundamental matrix for the system?" :question/worked-solution "Given a linear system ${x}^{′}=Ax,$ where $A$ is an $n\\times n$ matrix, a *fundamental matrix* $Φ(t)$ of the system is an $n\\times n$ matrix whose columns are linearly independent solutions of the system.\n\nIn our case, we have a $2\\times 2$ system. We are also given that the two linearly independent solutions of the system are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}=\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}=\\begin{bmatrix}2t{e}^{-t} \\\\ {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our fundamental could be written as\n\n$$\nΦ(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{-t} & 2t{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** The columns of $Φ$ can be written in any order."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365673 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221422 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "66c99cda-25a5-50f2-903b-bfb1fdc5fa10" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Fundamental Matrices Using Matrix Exponentials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5728e179-6831-5401-a928-e641db0a06b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365675}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5728e179-6831-5401-a928-e641db0a06b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365675} :answer-field/id #uuid "afbc2e57-8b1b-5ce4-9b27-cb6cab8bfe1d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365674} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "979acb26-7ddd-5b55-9211-9925729aa071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365677}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "979acb26-7ddd-5b55-9211-9925729aa071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365677} :answer-field/id #uuid "43739f8d-9cc2-5a3f-8f44-3fd681edf8db" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365676} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8029db2d-8bde-50dd-9836-f39ca854a3bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t{e}^{-4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365679}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8029db2d-8bde-50dd-9836-f39ca854a3bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t{e}^{-4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365679} :answer-field/id #uuid "faaddf78-a1db-5496-a708-d82d0e79d47b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365678} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6356927b-4df6-547a-97be-5637f834898e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365681}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6356927b-4df6-547a-97be-5637f834898e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365681} :answer-field/id #uuid "c911bdd1-5a68-53a2-90c8-77d2d0783bc8" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365680}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "93c5b9c1-d3c4-52c5-af95-03ae43585aaf" :question/math-academy-id "q-334497" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs ${x}^{′}=Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n| ${Φ}_{0}(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & {\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 0 & -4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The only eigenvalue of $At$ is \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-4t\n$$\n\nof multiplicity two. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}r(-4t) &={e}^{-4t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\lambda }r(-4t) &={e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0} &={e}^{-4t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1} &={e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{-4t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first one, we get\n\n$$\n-4t{e}^{-4t}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-4t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-4t}(1+4t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =-4{e}^{-4t}t+{e}^{-4t}(1+4t) \\\\  & ={e}^{-4t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}t & ={e}^{-4t}t \\\\  & =t{e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-4t} & t{e}^{-4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-4t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}]}
